    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on some dark chocolate batches and I keep running into an issue with fat bloom. The surface gets this dull, white streaky look after it sits for a bit. Can you explain what's actually happening chemically and how I can fix my tempering process to stop it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fat bloom occurs when cocoa butter within the chocolate recrystallizes onto the surface due to temperature fluctuations. This migration of fat molecules forms the unsightly white streaks you are observing. To prevent this, ensure your tempering process maintains a stable temperature profile, correctly hitting the critical crystallization points of cocoa butter. Slow, controlled cooling is key to encouraging stable, uniform crystal formation.",
      "source": "target",
